# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of the machine that cloned
# it.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its *system* configuration, so
# every Windows clone inherits it unless something says otherwise. Until
# Realisation XI nothing did, and the only thing standing between this tree and a
# wholesale CRLF rewrite was an untracked `core.autocrlf=false` set by hand in one
# working copy's `.git/config` — which no fresh clone gets, and which nobody
# reviewing a pull request would ever see was missing.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` settles it in the repository itself: Git decides what is text
# by inspecting it, normalises to LF on commit, and checks LF back out on every
# platform. This project is Linux-native and its build is scripted for a shell;
# `build/pacman-post-upgrade.sh`, the `.desktop` entry and the audit scripts all
# read their line endings as content — a CRLF in the scriptlet is a scriptlet
# `/bin/bash` refuses. Since Windows 10 1809 even Notepad reads LF, so there is no
# longer anything on the Windows side asking for the other convention.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Images are the only binaries tracked here — the hicolor set, the two marks the
# renderer imports, and the icons packaging cuts from. Declared so Git never
# inspects them for text, never diffs them as lines, and never rewrites a byte.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.svg text eol=lf

# Captured provider frames. These are evidence, not source: bytes that arrived
# from `hrmsocketonly.haremaltin.com` on a stated day, kept so a test can assert
# against what the source really sends rather than against what somebody
# believed it sends. `-text` rather than `binary` because a diff is still wanted
# if anyone edits one — but no normalisation, ever. A CRLF rewrite on a Windows
# checkout would not break the parse, and that is precisely the hazard: it would
# quietly make the fixture something other than what came off the wire.
tests/fixtures/**/*.frame -text

# The vault's own formats, in case a fixture is ever committed: an encrypted
# database and a backup container are ciphertext, and a single "corrected" byte
# is the difference between a restore and a loss.
*.db binary
*.jbk binary
*.keys binary

# Compiled native addons, if one is ever vendored deliberately.
*.node binary
